"""Comparison notes of the mini course's grammar pages (D18), frozen from the real course on 2026-10-09."""
import json
NOTES = json.loads(r"""{"ar": {"fn.definite": [{"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "English the and a become a prefix and an ending: الْبَيْتُ (the house), بَيْتٌ (a house). The fossil al- lives on in English words taken from Arabic: alcohol, algebra, alchemy."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "similar", "text": "Spanish loans keep the Arabic article — with the sun-letter assimilation: aceite (الزَّيْت az-zayt), azúcar (السُّكَّر as-sukkar), arroz (الرُّزّ ar-ruzz, a variant of أَرُزّ). Spanish has un / una for “a”; Arabic uses the ending -un."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "similar", "text": "French le / la and un / une become a prefix and an ending: الْبَيْتُ / بَيْتٌ. There is no partitive du / de la: أُرِيدُ قَهْوَةً is “I want (some) coffee”."}, {"for": "el", "rel": "similar", "text": "Greek has ο / η / το and ένας / μία / ένα; Arabic has one prefix for “the” (الْ, the same for every gender, number and case) and no separate “a”: the ending -un says it."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "new", "text": "Russian has no articles; Arabic marks every noun: الْبَيْتُ (the house) or بَيْتٌ (a house). Ask: do we both know which one? Then الْ."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "new", "text": "Hindi has no articles (एक means “one”); Arabic marks every noun as definite (الْ) or indefinite (-un)."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "similar", "text": "Persian marks “a” with an ending too (کتابی, a book) but has no “the” — only -rā on a definite object; Arabic puts الْ on every definite noun."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "new", "text": "Marathi has no articles; Arabic marks every noun as definite or indefinite."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "new", "text": "Turkish has bir for “a” and no “the” — only the -ı of a definite object; Arabic puts الْ on every definite noun, subject or object."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "new", "text": "Japanese has no articles; Arabic marks every noun: الْبَيْتُ or بَيْتٌ."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "new", "text": "Finnish has no articles; Arabic marks every noun, and a noun meant in general takes الْ: أُحِبُّ الْقَهْوَةَ (I like coffee)."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hungarian has a / az and egy and uses the article for things in general too (szeretem a kávét); Arabic does this with الْ, and says “a” with the ending -un."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "new", "text": "Swahili has no articles; Arabic marks every noun as definite (الْ) or indefinite (-un)."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "new", "text": "Luganda has no articles; Arabic marks every noun as definite (الْ) or indefinite (-un)."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "new", "text": "Vietnamese has no articles (một means “one”); Arabic marks every noun: الْبَيْتُ or بَيْتٌ."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "new", "text": "Inuktitut has no articles; Arabic marks every noun as definite (الْ) or indefinite (-un)."}], "fn.overview": [{"for": "el", "rel": "similar", "text": "Familiar: grammatical gender, agreement, case endings (Arabic has three: -u, -a, -i, roughly your nominative, accusative and genitive) and an article for things in general: أُحِبُّ الْقَهْوَةَ = μου αρέσει ο καφές. Arabic ث and ذ are your θ and δ, and the old letter order أ ب ج د matches α β γ δ; قَلَم (pen) goes back to κάλαμος. New: no neuter, no word for “a”, no capital letters, only three vowel qualities (a, i, u — short and long), sounds such as ع، ح، ق, and one past form for έφαγα and έχω φάει."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "different", "text": "Easy: one definite article (الْ) and many shared words — algebra, alcohol, sugar, coffee, cotton came into English from or through Arabic. Hard: a new script with no capital letters, two genders that also mark “you”, person endings on every verb, three cases, the dual, and short vowels that are not written. Arabic does not reduce unstressed vowels (كِتَابٌ is ki-tā-bun), has no phrasal verbs (خَرَجَ = go out), and one past form covers “I ate” and “I have eaten”."}, {"for": "de", "rel": "similar", "text": "You already learn nouns with their gender and plural and verbs with person endings — the same habits work here. Like German, Arabic needs no article with a job: Ich bin Lehrer = أَنَا مُعَلِّمٌ. The ach-Laut is Arabic خ, and the glottal stop before a vowel (be-achten) is the hamza ء. Different: no “is” in the present, two genders, no capital letters, no ü or ö (three vowel qualities only), no final devoicing (the d of وَلَد stays d), no separable prefixes, one past form for ich aß and ich habe gegessen, and no verb-second rule: the verb may come first (VSO) and never moves in a subordinate clause."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "similar", "text": "Russian has no present “to be” either: Я студент = أَنَا طَالِبٌ, word for word; the past shows the gender, as был / была = كَانَ / كَانَتْ; and “I have” is “at me (is)”: у меня есть ≈ عِنْدِي. Russian х is Arabic خ. Different: Arabic has a definite article (الْ), three cases instead of six, no capital letters, no hard / soft consonant pairs, no vowel reduction (every vowel keeps its sound), no final devoicing and no aspect prefixes on verbs."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Thousands of Turkish words are Arabic — kitap (كِتَاب), kalem (قَلَم), merhaba (مَرْحَبًا), selam (سَلَام), sabah (صَبَاح), insan (إِنْسَان), muallim (مُعَلِّم), saat (سَاعَة): your best bridge, and Ottoman Turkish was written in this script until 1928. Like Turkish, Arabic puts possessive endings on nouns (كِتَابِي ≈ kitabım) and needs no separate “to be” in the present. Hard: gender everywhere, even on “you”; changes inside the word instead of a chain of suffixes; no vowel harmony (endings keep their vowels) and no ü or ö; no final devoicing (kitap, but كِتَاب with b); no capital letters; few compounds like yardım etmek — “to help” is one verb, سَاعَدَ; no evidential or surprise -miş; and no -ip / -ince converbs — clauses are joined with words like وَ and عِنْدَمَا."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hundreds of Spanish words come from Arabic, many with the article al- fused in: aceite (الزَّيْت), azúcar (السُّكَّر), arroz (الْأَرُزّ), alcachofa (الْخُرْشُوف), berenjena (بَاذِنْجَان), and ojalá (لَوْ شَاءَ اللّٰهُ). Familiar: two genders, agreement, adjectives after the noun (casa grande = بَيْتٌ كَبِيرٌ). New: three cases, the dual, “you” by gender, no capital letters, no verb “to be” in the present (so no ser / estar choice), three vowel qualities instead of five, and one past form for comí and he comido."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Familiar: two genders and no neuter, the article for things in general (j’aime le café = أُحِبُّ الْقَهْوَةَ), adjectives mostly after the noun, and chez, which is Arabic عِنْدَ (chez le médecin = عِنْدَ الطَّبِيبِ). French took words from Arabic too: sucre, café, coton, épinard, aubergine, artichaut. New: three cases, the dual, “you” by gender, no “to be” in the present, no capital letters, and no nasal vowels or ü / eu — Arabic has three vowel qualities, short and long."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hindi took many Arabic words through Persian: किताब kitāb (كِتَاب — feminine in Hindi, masculine in Arabic), दुनिया (دُنْيَا), हाल (حَال, as in كَيْفَ حَالُكَ), लेकिन (لٰكِنْ). Familiar: two genders, “I have” as “near me is” (मेरे पास … है ≈ عِنْدِي) and a question word at the start (क्या ≈ هَلْ). New: prepositions instead of postpositions, three cases on the noun itself, the verb first, and a script read right to left. Arabic has no retroflex or aspirated consonants and no nasal vowels — its ṭ ḍ ṣ ẓ are emphatic (said with the back of the tongue raised), not retroflex —, and few करना-type compounds: “to work” is one verb, عَمِلَ."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "similar", "text": "Persian is written in the same script (Persian adds پ چ ژ گ) and is full of Arabic words — کتاب, وقت, حال, صبح — so reading will come fast. But Persian says several Arabic letters alike (ث س ص all s; ذ ز ض ظ all z; ح ه both h; ع like a glottal stop), and Arabic keeps them all apart. New: two genders, three cases, the dual, the article الْ, verbs that show the subject’s gender, and few compounds with کردن: Arabic uses single verbs (عَمِلَ, “to work”)."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "different", "text": "Marathi is written left to right in an abugida; Arabic is an abjad written right to left, with short vowels mostly unwritten. Familiar: grammatical gender (two, not three — no neuter), agreeing adjectives, and possession as “at me” (माझ्याकडे … आहे ≈ عِنْدِي). New: prepositions before the noun instead of postpositions, the verb first, no retroflex and no aspirated consonants, and only one “we”: نَحْنُ covers both आम्ही and आपण."}, {"for": "he", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hebrew is the closest relative: the same roots (כ־ת־ב / ك ت ب), the same two genders, the article as a prefix (הַ / الْ), no word for “a”, no “to be” in the present, and “I have” with a preposition and a pronoun (יֵשׁ לִי ≈ لِي, عِنْدِي). New in Arabic: three cases, the dual for every noun, broken plurals, plurals of things agreeing as feminine singular, and the sounds ع ح ق ط ص ض ظ, which keep their old, distinct values."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "different", "text": "Almost everything is new: letters joined right to left instead of kana and kanji, prepositions before the noun instead of particles after it, the verb first, gender on nouns, verbs and “you”, and plurals that must be marked. Familiar: long and short vowels and double consonants change the meaning (كَتَبَ / كَتَّبَ), as in Japanese. Arabic has three vowel qualities (a, i, u), no pitch accent, no counters (ثَلَاثَةُ كُتُبٍ, three books), few する-type verbs (“to study” is one verb, دَرَسَ), no -te forms, no hearsay forms like 〜そうだ, and no honorific verbs or speech levels: politeness lies in words such as مِنْ فَضْلِكَ and in titles."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "different", "text": "New: an alphabet joined right to left, gender, the verb first, prepositions instead of particles, and plurals that must be marked. Familiar: like 아니다, Arabic has a separate word for “is not” (لَيْسَ). Arabic has no aspirated series (ㅋ ㅌ ㅍ), no counters (세 권 → ثَلَاثَةُ كُتُبٍ), few 하다-type verbs (“to study” is one verb, دَرَسَ), no -고 / -서 clause chains, no endings for hearsay or surprise (-대, -네), and no honorific verbs or speech levels: politeness comes from words and titles."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "different", "text": "Familiar: possessive endings (kirjani ≈ كِتَابِي), “I have” as “at me” (minulla on ≈ عِنْدِي), and long vowels and double consonants that change the meaning. New: gender, even on “you”; prepositions instead of case endings; changes inside the word, but no consonant gradation; no vowel harmony (endings keep their vowels); no ä, ö, y — three vowel qualities only —; and no capital letters."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "different", "text": "Familiar: a definite article before the noun even after “this” — ez a ház = هٰذَا الْبَيْتُ, “this the house” —, possessive endings (házam ≈ بَيْتِي), an object that is always marked (házat ≈ الْبَيْتَ), and long vowels. New: gender, prepositions instead of case endings, the verb first, no vowel harmony, no ü / ö, no preverbs like meg- or el-, and no capital letters."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "similar", "text": "Swahili took many words from Arabic: kitabu (كِتَاب), saa (سَاعَة), wakati (وَقْت), mwalimu (مُعَلِّم), habari (خَبَر), lakini (لٰكِنْ), and the day names Alhamisi (الْخَمِيس) and Ijumaa (الْجُمُعَة); it was long written in Arabic script. Familiar: the adjective after the noun (kitabu kikubwa ≈ كِتَابٌ كَبِيرٌ), agreement everywhere, and a question word at the start (Je …? ≈ هَلْ). New: two sex-based genders instead of noun classes, endings and inner changes instead of prefixes, no applicative verbs (-ia, -ea), no capital letters, and three vowel qualities instead of five."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "different", "text": "Familiar: agreement that runs through the sentence, and long vowels and double consonants that change the meaning (Luganda writes them double; Arabic marks a double consonant with ـّ). New: two sex-based genders instead of noun classes — هُوَ he and هِيَ she where Luganda has ye for both —, changes inside the word and endings instead of prefixes, no applicative verbs, no capital letters, and no tones: pitch never changes the meaning of a word."}, {"for": "zh", "rel": "different", "text": "Almost everything is new: letters for sounds, words that change inside, gender, cases, plurals and agreement. Easier: no tones — pitch does not change a word —, no measure words (numbers stand right before the noun), no separable verb–object words like 吃饭, and no serial verbs. Arabic has one “we” (نَحْنُ) for both 我们 and 咱们, distinguishes voiced and voiceless (د d / ت t) rather than aspirated and plain, and has no retroflex sounds and no ü: ص and ط are emphatic, not zh / ch."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "different", "text": "New: letters joined right to left, words that change inside, gender, cases, plurals and agreement. Familiar: the adjective after the noun (nhà lớn ≈ بَيْتٌ كَبِيرٌ), the owner after the thing (sách của tôi ≈ كِتَابِي) and no “is” before an adjective. Arabic has no tones, no classifiers, no implosive ɓ / ɗ, no aspirated th set against t, no serial verbs, no polite particles, no capital letters, and one “we” (نَحْنُ) for both chúng ta and chúng tôi."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "different", "text": "Familiar: the dual (two of anything has its own form), possessive endings on nouns, person endings that make pronouns unnecessary, and the uvular q (Arabic ق). New: short separate words instead of one long word — no noun incorporation, no antipassive, no applicatives —, prepositions, gender, subjects marked the same way with every verb (no ergative), no evidential endings such as -guuq (“reportedly”), sentences joined with conjunctions instead of verb moods, and a script read right to left."}], "fn.plural.noun": [{"for": "el", "rel": "different", "text": "Greek plurals change the ending (βιβλίο → βιβλία); Arabic most often changes the inside of the word (كِتَاب → كُتُب), has a dual for two, and treats plurals of things as feminine singular."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "similar", "text": "Russian has unit nouns like the Arabic ones: горох → горошина (a pea), картофель → картофелина (a potato) — compare بَصَل → بَصَلَة (an onion). New are the dual and the broken plurals."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "different", "text": "Spanish adds -s / -es to every noun; most Arabic plurals are broken — a new vowel pattern inside the word (قَلَم → أَقْلَام) — and are learned with each noun."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "English has a few inside-the-word plurals (foot → feet, man → men); in Arabic that is the most common kind (رَجُل → رِجَال), and there is a dual for two."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "different", "text": "Hindi plurals change the ending by gender (लड़का → लड़के, किताब → किताबें); Arabic often changes the inside of the word and has a dual."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "similar", "text": "Persian uses Arabic broken plurals for many Arabic loanwords (کتب, اخبار) besides its own -hā / -ān, and even the Arabic ending -āt (سبزیجات, vegetables); in Arabic these are the normal plurals."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "different", "text": "Turkish has one plural ending, -ler / -lar, and drops it after numbers; Arabic has three kinds of plural plus a dual, learned with each noun."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "new", "text": "Japanese nouns usually do not mark plural; Arabic must choose singular, dual or plural every time."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "different", "text": "Finnish plurals are regular (-t, -i-); Arabic plurals are mostly broken and are learned with each noun."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "different", "text": "Hungarian adds -k (könyvek); Arabic mostly changes the vowels inside the word (كُتُب)."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "similar", "text": "Swahili makes the plural by changing the prefix (kitabu → vitabu), Arabic often by changing the vowels inside (كِتَاب → كُتُب) — in both, learn the pair."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "similar", "text": "Luganda changes the class prefix (ekitabo → ebitabo), Arabic often changes the inside of the word — in both, learn the pair."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "new", "text": "Vietnamese marks plural only with words like các / những; Arabic marks it on the noun itself, with a dual for two."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "similar", "text": "Inuktitut also has singular, dual and plural: inuk, inuuk, inuit (one, two, several people) — like Arabic -āni for two; but Arabic plurals are often broken."}], "fn.root.pattern": [{"for": "el", "rel": "new", "text": "Greek builds words from stems and endings (γράφω, γραφείο, γράμμα); Arabic weaves three root consonants into vowel patterns (كَتَبَ, مَكْتَب, كِتَاب). أَرُزّ (rice) is the same word as ὄρυζα, borrowed through Aramaic — a loan with no usable root."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "new", "text": "Russian builds families with prefixes and suffixes on a stem (писать, писатель, письмо); Arabic changes the vowels and adds letters around a root of consonants (كَتَبَ, كَاتِب, كِتَاب)."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "English keeps traces of the idea in sing / sang / song; in Arabic it is the main way to make words: ك ت ب → كَتَبَ, كَاتِب, كِتَاب, مَكْتَب."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "new", "text": "Spanish builds words with suffixes (cocinar, cocina, cocinero); Arabic with patterns around a root: طَبَخَ (cook), مَطْبَخ (kitchen), طَبَّاخ (a cook)."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "new", "text": "French cuisine, cuisinier add suffixes to a stem; Arabic puts the root ط ب خ into patterns: مَطْبَخ (kitchen), طَبَّاخ (cook)."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hindi borrowed whole Arabic word families through Persian — इल्म (knowledge), आलिम (scholar), मालूम (known) all come from ع ل م; in Arabic the patterns behind them are living rules."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "similar", "text": "Persian is full of Arabic words, so many patterns look familiar: کتاب, کاتب, مکتب (ک ت ب); علم, عالم, معلم (ع ل م). شَاي came into Arabic from Persian چای — a loan with no Arabic root."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "new", "text": "Marathi builds words with prefixes and suffixes on a stem; Arabic weaves a consonant root into patterns: ك ت ب → كِتَاب, كَاتِب, مَكْتَب."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Turkish already has whole Arabic root families: kitap, kâtip (clerk), mektup (letter), mektep (school) all come from ك ت ب; ilim, âlim, muallim, malumat from ع ل م. In Arabic you learn the patterns behind them."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "new", "text": "Japanese makes new words by combining kanji and adding endings; Arabic changes the vowels inside a consonant root."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "new", "text": "Finnish adds suffixes to an unchanging stem (kirja, kirjasto, kirjailija); Arabic keeps three consonants and changes everything around them (كِتَاب, مَكْتَبَة, كَاتِب)."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "new", "text": "Hungarian adds suffixes to an unchanging stem; Arabic changes the vowels inside the root."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "similar", "text": "Swahili borrowed Arabic word families too: kitabu (book), katibu (secretary), maktaba (library) — all ك ت ب. In Arabic the pattern is a living rule."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "new", "text": "Luganda builds words with class prefixes and verb extensions; Arabic changes the vowel pattern around a consonant root."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "new", "text": "Vietnamese words never change inside; new words are made by combining syllables. Arabic makes them by pouring a root into patterns."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "new", "text": "Inuktitut builds long words by adding suffix after suffix to a stem; Arabic keeps words short and changes the vowels and added letters around a root of three consonants."}]}, "he": {"fn.definite": [{"for": "el", "rel": "similar", "text": "Greek has a definite article too (ο / η / το), but Hebrew הַ never changes for gender, number or case, and it is written onto the word. There is no ένας / μία: סֵפֶר is “a book”."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "new", "text": "Russian has no articles; Hebrew marks “the” with הַ: סֵפֶר “a book, book” — הַסֵּפֶר “the book”. Use it whenever the listener knows which one."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "similar", "text": "Like el / la / los / las, but one form for all, written onto the noun: הַבַּיִת, הַדִּירָה, הַבָּתִּים. There is no un / una: a bare noun is indefinite."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "הַ works much like “the” and never changes; but there is no “a”: סֵפֶר is “a book”. And the article is repeated on a demonstrative (later on an adjective) after the noun: הַסֵּפֶר הַזֶּה."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Like le / la / les, but invariable and attached: הַבַּיִת. There is no un / une and no partitive du / des: אֲנִי אוֹכֵל לֶחֶם “I eat (some) bread”."}, {"for": "de", "rel": "similar", "text": "One article for der / die / das in every case: הַ. There is no ein / eine — a bare noun is indefinite — and the gender shows only in the words that agree."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "new", "text": "Hindi has no articles; Hebrew marks a thing the listener knows with הַ, written onto the noun."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "different", "text": "Persian has no definite article; it marks a definite object with را and an indefinite noun with -ی. Hebrew marks definiteness on the noun itself with הַ (and later puts אֶת before a definite object)."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "new", "text": "Marathi has no articles; Hebrew marks a known noun with the prefix הַ."}, {"for": "ar", "rel": "similar", "text": "The same idea as الْـ: a prefix, one form for all, and a doubled first letter after it (الشَّمْس ~ the dagesh in הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ). But Hebrew has no indefinite ending: كِتَابٌ (with tanwīn) is just סֵפֶר."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "different", "text": "Turkish has no “the”; it uses bir for “a” and marks a definite object with -(y)ı. Hebrew has the opposite tool: a “the” (הַ) and no “a”."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "new", "text": "Japanese has no articles; Hebrew adds הַ to every noun the listener can identify, and leaves it off otherwise."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "new", "text": "Finnish has no articles; Hebrew marks “the” with הַ — whenever the listener knows which one."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hungarian has a / az “the” and egy “a”; Hebrew has only “the”, written onto the word (הַבַּיִת), and no “a”."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "new", "text": "Swahili has no articles; Hebrew marks a definite noun with הַ."}, {"for": "zh", "rel": "new", "text": "Chinese has no articles (这 / 那 or the context show what is known); Hebrew uses הַ for every definite noun, and a bare noun means “a …”."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "new", "text": "Vietnamese has no articles; Hebrew puts הַ on every definite noun."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "new", "text": "Inuktitut has no articles; Hebrew marks a definite noun with the prefix הַ."}], "fn.overview": [{"for": "el", "rel": "similar", "text": "Easy: masculine and feminine nouns and agreement will feel natural, and the alphabet is a relative of yours — alpha, beta, gamma, delta come from the same Phoenician letters as א ב ג ד (aleph, bet, gimel, dalet). Different: no neuter (το βιβλίο is masculine סֵפֶר), only a definite article, and no polite plural “you” (no εσείς of respect). Greek drops subject pronouns everywhere; Hebrew drops them in the past and future but keeps them in the present (אֲנִי סְטוּדֶנְט). Sounds: Hebrew has no θ / δ — ת and ד are plain t and d."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "Easy: the word order is subject – verb – object, nouns have no case endings, and “the” is one invariable word (הַ). Hard: gender — in nouns, in “you” (אַתָּה / אַתְּ) and in the verb — the right-to-left script and reading without vowels. International words (טֶלֶפוֹן, סְטוּדֶנְט) are good first anchors. Sounds: no “th” — ת is t — and p, t, k are not aspirated."}, {"for": "de", "rel": "similar", "text": "Easy for a German speaker: ח and כ sound like the ch in Bach, the r is the back r of German, and you are used to learning every noun with its gender. Different: two genders, not three, and the article הַ does not show the gender — it shows in the words that agree (זֶה / זֹאת, הוּא / הִיא); German neuters land on either side (das Buch → סֵפֶר m, das Bett → מִטָּה f). There are no cases and no du / Sie. A final consonant keeps its voice (יֶלֶד ends in a real d, not t), and there is no ü or ö. Some Hebrew is calqued on German or Yiddish: בְּסֵדֶר = in Ordnung."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "similar", "text": "Your best head start: like Russian, Hebrew says “I student” with no verb in the present (אֲנִי סְטוּדֶנְט = я студент), and its past “to be” agrees in gender and number (הָיָה / הָיְתָה / הָיוּ like был / была / были). Different: Hebrew has an article (הַ, “the”) and no cases, and no polite вы. Sounds: there are no soft (palatalised) consonants, and final consonants are not devoiced (יֶלֶד ends in d). Israel has a large Russian-speaking community, so you will hear Russian too."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Turkish took many Arabic words, and many share their roots with Hebrew: selam ~ שָׁלוֹם, insan ~ אֲנָשִׁים “people”, kitap ~ the root כ־ת־ב “to write”. Different: Turkish has no gender — in Hebrew it is everywhere — and Turkish adds endings to say “I am” (öğretmenim), while Hebrew says nothing in the present (אֲנִי מוֹרֶה). Hebrew puts the verb before the object (SVO) and has an article. There is no vowel harmony (every ending has one form) and no ü or ö, and no evidential or surprise ending like -mış: הוּא אָכַל is “he ate”, whether you saw it or were told."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "similar", "text": "Two genders, as in Spanish, and adjectives that follow the noun and agree (casa grande ~ בַּיִת גָּדוֹל). New: no present “to be” at all — neither ser nor estar (אֲנִי סְטוּדֶנְט, הַסֵּפֶר פֹּה); the right-to-left consonant script; and no usted. Sounds: no θ (Castilian z) and no soft d: ד is always d."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Two genders, as in French, and the back r (ר) is close to the French r. Different: the article הַ is a prefix and does not show gender (le / la → הַ), there is no un / une and no partitive, no vous of politeness, and no present “to be”. Sounds: no nasal vowels and no front rounded u or eu — just a, e, i, o, u."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "similar", "text": "Familiar: two genders and a verb that shows the gender of the subject — also for “I” (मैं जाता हूँ / मैं जाती हूँ), so אֲנִי הוֹלֵךְ / אֲנִי הוֹלֶכֶת will feel natural. Different: Hebrew is SVO with prepositions, not SOV with postpositions; it has no present “to be” (Hindi हूँ / है); and no आप / तुम politeness. Sounds: no retroflex consonants and no aspirated stops — p, t, k differ from b, d, g by voice alone."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "similar", "text": "Familiar: a right-to-left script that leaves out short vowels, and many Arabic loans whose roots Hebrew shares (کتاب ~ כ־ת־ב). New: grammatical gender, which Persian lacks, a definite article (הַ), and no present “to be”; Hebrew also has no polite شما."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Familiar: grammatical gender and verbs that change with the speaker’s gender (मी जातो / मी जाते “I go”) — the Hebrew present does the same (אֲנִי הוֹלֵךְ / אֲנִי הוֹלֶכֶת). New: a right-to-left consonant script, prepositions instead of endings and postpositions, no neuter, and no copula in the present (Marathi आहे). Sounds: no retroflex and no aspirated consonants."}, {"for": "ar", "rel": "similar", "text": "Your closest relative: the same root-and-pattern system (כ־ת־ב ~ ك ت ب, שׁ־ל־ם ~ س ل م), a right-to-left abjad, a verbless present and an article prefix (הַ ~ الْـ). Simpler in Hebrew: no case endings, only a few duals, and plural things take plural agreement. Sounds: in Modern Hebrew ח is [χ] and ע is weak or silent — not pharyngeal — and ט, צ, ק are plain t, ts, k, not emphatic."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "new", "text": "Almost everything is new: two genders, an article, prepositions before the noun and the order subject – verb – object instead of particles after the noun and the verb at the end. Hebrew has no speech levels — politeness is in words like בְּבַקָּשָׁה — and no pitch accent: stress, mostly on the last syllable, is what matters. The dagesh never makes a consonant long, so there are no double consonants like っ."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "new", "text": "New: gender everywhere, an article, prepositions and SVO order; no honorific speech levels — Hebrew politeness is in words, not in verb endings. Sounds: no three-way contrast like ㄱ / ㅋ / ㄲ — Hebrew separates p, t, k from b, d, g by voice."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "new", "text": "New: grammatical gender (hän is both he and she; Hebrew splits even “you”), an article, and no cases — prepositions do what Finnish endings do (-ssa, -lle, -sta). Familiar: “have” is said “to me is” (יֵשׁ לִי ~ minulla on). Sounds: no vowel harmony, no ä / ö / y, no long vowels or double consonants, and no consonant gradation."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "new", "text": "Familiar: a definite article (a / az ~ הַ) and “have” as “to me is” (nekem van ~ יֵשׁ לִי). New: grammatical gender (ő is both he and she), prepositions instead of case suffixes, and no present “to be” in any person. Sounds: no vowel harmony and no ö / ü, no long vowels."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "similar", "text": "Swahili noun classes prepare you for agreement: every Hebrew noun is masculine or feminine, and adjectives, demonstratives and verbs agree with it, as they agree with a class in Swahili. Different: only two classes, decided by sex or by the ending, and marked with endings (-ָה, -ִים, -וֹת) instead of prefixes; Swahili also has Arabic loans that share Hebrew roots (kitabu ~ כ־ת־ב)."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "similar", "text": "Luganda noun classes and agreement are good preparation: Hebrew agreement works the same way, with only two genders and with endings rather than prefixes. New: no tones — Hebrew pitch is only intonation — and a right-to-left consonant script."}, {"for": "zh", "rel": "new", "text": "Almost all the grammar is new: endings for gender and number, agreement, an article, and verbs that change. Familiar: subject – verb – object order and no verb “to be” before an adjective (הוּא גָּבֹהַּ ~ 他很高). Sounds: no tones — pitch is only intonation. Hebrew needs no measure words, no serial verbs (“go to buy” is הוֹלֵךְ לִקְנוֹת, with an infinitive) and no dummy objects like 吃饭: אֲנִי אוֹכֵל is enough."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "new", "text": "Familiar: subject – verb – object and adjectives after the noun (nhà lớn ~ בַּיִת גָּדוֹל). New: inflection for gender and number, an article, and words that change shape inside (roots in patterns). Sounds: no tones — pitch never changes the meaning of a word. Verbs are not strung together: “go to buy” is הוֹלֵךְ לִקְנוֹת, with an infinitive."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "new", "text": "Hebrew builds words inside the word too (roots in patterns, prefixes such as בְּ, לְ, הַ), but far less: a Hebrew sentence is several separate words where Inuktitut may use one, and an object is never built into the verb (אֲנִי אוֹכֵל לֶחֶם). New: two genders, an article, prepositions instead of case endings, and accusative rather than ergative alignment."}, {"for": "zu", "rel": "new", "text": "Hebrew has no click consonants; its sounds are all ordinary pulmonic consonants and five vowels, and there is no tone."}, {"for": "am", "rel": "different", "text": "Amharic’s ejectives (ጥ, ቅ …) have no Hebrew counterpart: ט and ק were once special consonants, but today they are plain t and k. Like Amharic, Hebrew is Semitic, with roots and patterns and two genders."}, {"for": "cy", "rel": "different", "text": "Hebrew ב, כ, פ switch between b / v, k / kh, p / f (בַּיִת “house” — בְּבַיִת “in a house”, said bevayit), but this follows the position of the sound in the word, not grammar — it is not a mutation like the Welsh ones."}], "fn.plural.noun": [{"for": "el", "rel": "similar", "text": "As in Greek, the plural ending depends on the noun (-οι, -ες, -α ~ -ִים, -וֹת) and must be learnt with it; Hebrew adds vowel changes inside the stem (סֵפֶר → סְפָרִים)."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "similar", "text": "As in Russian, the ending depends on the noun and the stem can change; Hebrew has just two main endings, -ִים and -וֹת, and no case forms of the plural."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "different", "text": "Spanish adds -s / -es to every noun; Hebrew chooses between -ִים and -וֹת, often changes the stem vowels (סֵפֶר → סְפָרִים) and keeps a dual in a few words (יוֹמַיִם)."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "different", "text": "English adds -s and has a few irregulars (man → men); in Hebrew stem changes are common (סֵפֶר → סְפָרִים, בַּיִת → בָּתִּים), so learn the plural with the word."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "different", "text": "The French plural is mostly silent (-s); the Hebrew plural is always heard: -ִים / -וֹת, often with a vowel change in the stem."}, {"for": "de", "rel": "similar", "text": "As in German, the plural is learnt with each noun and can change the stem (Buch → Bücher ~ סֵפֶר → סְפָרִים); Hebrew has only two endings, but many stem changes."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hindi plurals depend on gender and ending (लड़का → लड़के, लड़की → लड़कियाँ), as Hebrew ones do (-ִים / -וֹת); Hebrew also changes vowels inside the stem."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "different", "text": "The Persian endings -ها / -ان do not depend on gender, and the noun stays singular after a number; the Hebrew ending depends on the noun, and the plural is used after every number from 2."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Marathi plurals depend on gender and ending (मुलगा → मुलगे); Hebrew too — -ִים mostly for masculine, -וֹת mostly for feminine — with stem changes."}, {"for": "ar", "rel": "similar", "text": "The sound plurals -ūna / -āt match -ִים / -וֹת, but Hebrew has no broken plurals: the inside of the word changes only its vowels (סֵפֶר → סְפָרִים, not like كِتَاب → كُتُب), and the dual lives only in a few words."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "different", "text": "Turkish has one plural suffix (-ler / -lar, by vowel harmony) and drops it after numbers (üç kitap); Hebrew has two endings, stem changes, and a plural after every number (שְׁלוֹשָׁה סְפָרִים)."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "new", "text": "Japanese nouns usually have no plural form; in Hebrew the plural is obligatory — סֵפֶר “a book”, סְפָרִים “books” — and everything that agrees becomes plural too."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "new", "text": "Korean marks the plural only optionally (-들); in Hebrew it is obligatory whenever there is more than one."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "different", "text": "Finnish marks the plural with -t or with -i- before a case ending; Hebrew uses -ִים / -וֹת and often changes the stem vowels."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "different", "text": "Hungarian adds -k (könyvek) but keeps the singular after numbers; Hebrew uses -ִים / -וֹת and always the plural after numbers."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "different", "text": "Swahili makes the plural by changing the class prefix (kitabu → vitabu, mtu → watu); Hebrew changes the ending (סֵפֶר → סְפָרִים) and often the stem vowels."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "different", "text": "Luganda changes the class prefix for the plural (omuntu → abantu, ekitabo → ebitabo); Hebrew changes the ending: -ִים / -וֹת."}, {"for": "zh", "rel": "new", "text": "Chinese nouns do not change for number (们 only for people); in Hebrew every countable noun has a plural, and it must be used: שְׁנֵי סְפָרִים."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "new", "text": "Vietnamese nouns do not change for number (những / các show it when needed); Hebrew nouns always take -ִים / -וֹת for more than one."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "different", "text": "Inuktitut has singular, dual and plural endings for every noun (iglu, igluk, iglut); Hebrew has the plural for all nouns and the dual only for a few (יוֹמַיִם, עֵינַיִם)."}], "fn.root.pattern": [{"for": "ar", "rel": "same", "text": "The same system, often with the same roots: א־כ־ל ~ ء ك ل (أَكَلَ), כ־ת־ב ~ ك ت ب, שׁ־ל־ם ~ س ل م. The binyanim match Arabic verb forms: piʿel ~ form II with a doubled middle letter, hifʿil ~ form IV."}, {"for": "el", "rel": "different", "text": "Greek builds words from a stem plus endings and suffixes (γράφω, γράμμα, γραφείο); Hebrew pours three consonants into vowel patterns: א־כ־ל → אָכַל, אוֹכֵל, אֹכֶל."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "different", "text": "Russian adds prefixes and suffixes to a stem (писать, писатель, написать); Hebrew keeps the consonants and changes the vowels and pattern letters around them."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "different", "text": "Spanish derives with suffixes (cocinar, cocina, cocinero); Hebrew changes the vowel pattern of the root ב־שׁ־ל: בִּשֵּׁל, מְבַשֵּׁל, בִּשּׁוּל."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "English sing – sang – song is a small taste of the idea: the consonants stay and the vowels change. In Hebrew this is the main way of making words."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "different", "text": "French builds words with suffixes on a stem (cuire, cuisson, cuisine); Hebrew puts a consonant root into patterns."}, {"for": "de", "rel": "similar", "text": "German singen – sang – gesungen – Gesang changes vowels inside a frame of consonants, as Hebrew does everywhere: אָכַל, אוֹכֵל, אֹכֶל."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "different", "text": "Hindi words grow from stems with suffixes; its Arabic loans (किताब) share roots with Hebrew (כ־ת־ב), but Hindi does not change their vowels to make new words."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "similar", "text": "Persian borrowed many Arabic words together with their patterns (کتاب، کاتب، مکتب), so the root idea is familiar: Hebrew כְּתָב, כּוֹתֵב, מִכְתָּב are built in the same way from כ־ת־ב."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "different", "text": "Marathi words grow from stems with suffixes; Hebrew words grow from roots of three consonants placed into vowel patterns."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "different", "text": "Turkish keeps the stem and adds suffixes in a row (yaz-, yazar, yazı); Hebrew changes the vowels inside a root and adds letters around it (כ־ת־ב: כָּתַב, כְּתָב, מִכְתָּב). The Arabic loans kitap, katip, mektup come from the same root."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "different", "text": "Japanese builds words with suffixes and compounds (食べる, 食べ物); Hebrew changes the vowels around a root: אָכַל “he ate”, אֹכֶל “food”."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "different", "text": "Korean adds endings to an unchanging stem (먹다, 먹어요); Hebrew keeps only the consonants fixed: אָכַל, אוֹכֵל, אֹכֶל."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "different", "text": "Finnish adds suffixes and changes consonants at the edge of the stem; Hebrew keeps the root consonants and changes the vowels between them."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "different", "text": "Hungarian chains suffixes onto a stem; Hebrew changes the vowel pattern around a root."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "different", "text": "Swahili keeps the verb root and adds prefixes and suffixes (kupika “to cook”, mpishi “a cook”); Hebrew changes the vowels inside: בִּשֵּׁל, מְבַשֵּׁל, בִּשּׁוּל. The Arabic loan kitabu shares the root כ־ת־ב."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "different", "text": "Luganda builds words with class prefixes and verb extensions around a fixed root; Hebrew changes the vowels around three consonants."}, {"for": "zh", "rel": "different", "text": "Chinese never changes the inside of a word; it joins whole syllables (吃 + 饭 → 吃饭). Hebrew changes the vowels and adds letters around a root: אָכַל, אוֹכֵל, אֹכֶל."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "different", "text": "Vietnamese words never change inside; new words are compounds of whole syllables. Hebrew makes new words by changing the vowels around a root."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "different", "text": "Inuktitut builds long words by adding suffix after suffix to a stem; Hebrew keeps words short and changes their inside — the vowels around the root consonants."}]}, "zh": {"fn.definite": [{"for": "el", "rel": "different", "text": "Greek uses ο / η / το for known things and ένας / μία / ένα for new ones; Chinese has no ‘the’, and 一 + measure word is the counterpart of ένας. Position does the rest: a known thing stands before the verb (书在这里)."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "same", "text": "Russian has no articles either and uses word order the same way: Книга на столе (the book) vs На столе книга (a book) — exactly 书在桌子上 vs 桌子上有一本书."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "different", "text": "Spanish el / la / un / una come with nearly every noun; Chinese has none — 一 + measure word only when ‘one’ or a new thing matters."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "different", "text": "English the / a are obligatory; Chinese has neither. Word order does the work of ‘the’: a known thing before the verb (书在这里 ‘the book is here’), a new thing after 有 (这里有书 ‘there is a book here’)."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "different", "text": "French le / la / les / un / une / du are obligatory; Chinese has none — 我喝咖啡 is je bois du café and je bois le café alike."}, {"for": "de", "rel": "different", "text": "German der / ein carry gender and case; Chinese has no article at all, and 一 + measure word appears only when ‘one’ matters."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hindi has no definite article either and uses एक like 一 + measure word for something new; Hindi marks a known object with को, which Chinese does not need."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "similar", "text": "Persian has no ‘the’; a new thing gets -i or yek (ketābi ‘a book’), a known object rā. Chinese has only 一 + measure word for ‘a’ and nothing for ‘the’ or a known object."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "same", "text": "Marathi has no articles; एक ‘one’ introduces a new thing, like Chinese 一 + measure word."}, {"for": "ar", "rel": "different", "text": "Arabic al- makes a noun definite and -un (tanwīn) often marks it indefinite; Chinese marks neither — position and 这 / 那 do the work."}, {"for": "he", "rel": "different", "text": "Hebrew ha- marks a known noun and repeats on its adjective (ha-bayit ha-gadol); Chinese has no article: 大房子 is ‘a big house’ or ‘the big house’ by context."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "same", "text": "Turkish has no ‘the’ and uses bir for ‘a’ — exactly 一 + measure word. A known object takes -ı in Turkish (kitabı okudum); Chinese marks it with nothing."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "same", "text": "Japanese has no articles either; the topic particle は marks known things, a role Chinese gives to the start of the sentence."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "same", "text": "Korean has no articles; 은 / 는 marks a known topic, which Chinese shows by putting it first."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "same", "text": "Finnish has no articles and uses word order like Chinese: Kirja on pöydällä ‘the book is on the table’ vs Pöydällä on kirja ‘there is a book on the table’."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "different", "text": "Hungarian has a / az and egy, like an article system; Chinese has neither — position does the work of ‘the’, and 一 + measure word that of ‘a’."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "same", "text": "Swahili has no articles; demonstratives or context make a noun definite, as in Chinese."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "same", "text": "Luganda has no article words; as in Chinese, context and position decide whether a noun is known."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "same", "text": "Vietnamese has no definite article either; một ‘one’ + classifier introduces a new thing (một cái bàn), just like 一张桌子."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "same", "text": "Inuktitut has no articles; neither has Chinese."}], "fn.overview": [{"for": "el", "rel": "different", "text": "Almost everything Greek inflects disappears: no genders, no cases, no verb endings, no articles. The new things are tones (the Greek accent is stress, not a pitch that changes the word) and measure words. A gift from Greek: Chinese b, d, g are unaspirated like Greek π, τ, κ, while p, t, k carry a strong puff of air, and Chinese h is close to Greek χ in χαρά. Chinese has no θ or δ, characters have no capital letters and no final forms like ς, and the speaker’s sex never shows (κουρασμένος / κουρασμένη — both 我累了)."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "The order subject – verb – object matches English (我是学生 ‘I am a student’), and English is already fairly analytic, so grammar without endings feels natural. Watch out for pinyin letters with values unlike English (q, x, zh, c, ü), for the missing ‘th’ sounds, and for English intonation: in Chinese the pitch belongs to each word, so a question is made with 吗, not by raising the voice. Characters have no capital letters; names are capitalised only in pinyin."}, {"for": "de", "rel": "similar", "text": "Your German compounds help a lot: Chinese builds words the same way (手机 ‘hand machine’ — the German Handy; 电话 ‘electric speech’). German ü is pinyin ü (女 nǚ), the ach-Laut is close to Chinese h, the ich-Laut close to x. Measure words have small German cousins — ein Blatt Papier, eine Tasse Kaffee, zwei Stück Kuchen — but in Chinese every counted noun needs one. Forget V2: the Chinese verb never moves to second place (明天我去 or 我明天去, never *明天去我). Syllables end only in a vowel, -n, -ng or -r, so there is nothing to devoice, and nouns are not capitalised — characters have no case."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "similar", "text": "Like Russian, Chinese has no articles. Russian drops ‘to be’ in the present (Я студент); Chinese needs 是 between two nouns (我是学生) but, like Russian, uses none before adjectives (她很高 ~ Она высокая). Russian ш is close to Chinese sh, щ to x, and your feel for verbal aspect (perfective / imperfective) will help with 了 and 过. New: no hard / soft consonant pairs, no cases, no word stress, no capital letters, syllables that end only in a vowel, -n, -ng or -r (nothing to devoice), and the speaker’s sex never shows (я устал / устала — both 我累了)."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Turkish is the opposite technique — long words built from suffixes — yet many habits carry over: no gender (o = he / she / it, just like spoken tā), no ‘the’ (bir works like 一 + measure word), no plural after a number (iki kitap ~ 两本书), modifiers before the noun, and a question particle (mı ~ 吗). Unlike Turkish suffixes, Chinese particles never follow vowel harmony: 吗 is always 吗. What remains different is the order — Chinese puts the verb before the object (SVO) — and Chinese has no evidential like -miş: ‘I hear that …’ is said with words. Characters have no capital letters, there are no double consonants, and syllables end only in a vowel, -n, -ng or -r."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "different", "text": "Spanish ser / estar prepare you for 是 / 在, and the SVO order matches. Everything else that changes in Spanish — gender, plural, verb endings, the subjunctive — is absent: a Chinese verb stays the same after ‘I want …’ or ‘so that …’. New are tones and measure words; characters have no capital letters, and Chinese has no θ."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "different", "text": "French is SVO like Chinese and has no strong word stress, which suits the even beat of Chinese syllables; but gender, articles, conjugation and the subjunctive all disappear. French nasal vowels have no Chinese counterpart: in an / ang the n and ng are real consonants. Pinyin ü is French u (女 nǚ), and Chinese r is not the French r from the back of the throat. Characters have no capital letters."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "different", "text": "Hindi’s aspirated / unaspirated pairs (प / फ, क / ख) are exactly Chinese b / p and g / k, and its retroflex consonants give a feel for the curled tongue of zh, ch, sh. But Chinese has no gender, no verb endings and no subject–object–verb order: the object follows the verb (我吃饭). Nasal vowels and double consonants do not exist, and the speaker’s sex never shows in the verb (मैं थका / थकी हूँ — both 我累了)."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "different", "text": "Persian has no gender either, and u is ‘he’ and ‘she’, as tā is in speech; Persian tā even works as an optional classifier (do tā ketāb ~ 两本书). New: tones; characters that never join or change shape; the object after the verb; and modifiers before the noun — the reverse of the ezafe (ketāb-e man ~ 我的书). Chinese has no uvular q and no double consonants: pinyin q is an aspirated, ‘ch’-like sound."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "different", "text": "Marathi’s aspirated / unaspirated pairs match Chinese b / p, d / t, g / k, and its आपण / आम्ही split is Chinese 咱们 / 我们. Like Marathi, Chinese puts modifiers before the noun and has a question particle (का ~ 吗). New: tones, no gender, no endings, the object after the verb, no double consonants — and the speaker’s sex never shows in the verb."}, {"for": "ar", "rel": "different", "text": "Arabic builds words from roots and patterns; Chinese builds them by compounding whole syllables (电 + 话 = 电话) and never changes a word inside. Characters do not join or change shape by position, and there is no dual, no gender and no case; Chinese needs 是 between two nouns where Arabic needs nothing. Pharyngeal and emphatic consonants, double consonants and long vowels play no role — tones do. Pinyin q is not Arabic q but an aspirated, ‘ch’-like sound, and there are no θ / ð sounds."}, {"for": "he", "rel": "different", "text": "Hebrew roots (k-t-v) have no Chinese counterpart: words are compounds of syllables (电 + 话 = 电话) and never change inside. Like Hebrew, Chinese puts no verb before adjectives (房子很大 ~ ha-bayit gadol), but it needs 是 between two nouns. Characters have no final forms (unlike ך ם ן ף ץ), and gender disappears even from ‘you’ (ata / at — 你)."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "similar", "text": "Japanese gives you a head start: kanji are Chinese characters (学生, 电话 ~ 電話), counters work like measure words, the topic comes first, and the question particle か matches 吗. New: tones instead of pitch accent, simplified character forms (话 for 話), and SVO order — the object follows the verb (我吃饭). Some kanji mislead: Japanese 手紙 is ‘letter’, Chinese 手纸 is toilet paper. Long vowels and double consonants (おばあさん, きって) play no role in Chinese."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "similar", "text": "Many Korean words are Sino-Korean and close to their Chinese source (학생 haksaeng ~ 学生 xuésheng, 전화 jeonhwa ~ 电话 diànhuà), and counters, topic-first order and verb-like adjectives are familiar. New: tones, characters instead of Hangul, SVO order (the object after the verb), and no speech levels — politeness is 您, 请 and titles, not verb endings."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "different", "text": "Finnish has no gender (hän ~ spoken tā) and no articles, but its fifteen cases, vowel harmony, consonant gradation and long vowels and consonants all disappear: a Chinese word never changes, and length means nothing. What is new is pitch — tones tell words apart. Characters have no capital letters."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hungarian puts the family name first (Kovács János ~ 王小明), writes dates big to small (2026. május 1. ~ 2026年五月一号), has no gender (ő ~ tā), keeps the noun singular after numbers (két könyv ~ 两本书) and even has two words for ‘two’ (két / kettő ~ 两 / 二). Cases, vowel harmony, long vowels and double consonants disappear, and characters have no capital letters; tones are new."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "different", "text": "Swahili is SVO, so the basic order is familiar, but Chinese tones are new. Noun classes and their agreement disappear — Chinese has measure words instead, which also sort nouns by kind. Chinese counts the hours from midnight, not from 6 a.m.; it has no ‘th’ sounds (as in thelathini), and characters have no capital letters."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "similar", "text": "Luganda is a tone language too, so pitch that tells words apart is familiar; Chinese tones are contours on every syllable. Noun classes, agreement and verb prefixes disappear — Chinese words never change, and measure words sort nouns by kind. The SVO order matches, but Chinese puts adjectives, numbers and possessors before the noun, Luganda after it. Characters have no capital letters."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "similar", "text": "Vietnamese is the closest type of all: isolating, tonal, SVO, with classifiers, verb-like adjectives and many Sino-Vietnamese words (học sinh ~ 学生, điện thoại ~ 电话). Two differences matter: Chinese puts modifiers before the noun (我的书, 大房子), Vietnamese after it (sách của tôi, nhà to); and Chinese is written in characters, with pinyin only as a learner’s aid; characters have no capital letters."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "different", "text": "Inuktitut is the opposite extreme: one long word can be a whole sentence, built from a stem and many suffixes. Chinese words are short and never change; every meaning is a separate word in a fixed order. Verb–object words such as 吃饭 ‘eat-meal’ look like incorporation but are two words that can be split (吃了饭). Pinyin q is not the Inuktitut uvular q but an aspirated, ‘ch’-like sound."}], "fn.plural.noun": [{"for": "el", "rel": "new", "text": "Greek marks the plural on noun, article and adjective (το βιβλίο → τα βιβλία); Chinese marks it nowhere — 书 is one book or many, and 们 is only for groups of people."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "new", "text": "Russian marks plural and case together (книга → книги, after five книг); Chinese 书 never changes."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "new", "text": "Spanish adds -s / -es to noun, article and adjective (los libros nuevos); Chinese adds nothing: 新书 is ‘new book’ or ‘new books’."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "trap", "text": "English adds -s even after numbers (three books); Chinese never: 三本书. 们 is not -s: it is only for people (孩子们) and never after a number."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "new", "text": "French plural is mostly silent in speech (livre / livres) but shown by the article (le / les); Chinese shows it with neither — only context, numbers, 这些 and 很多."}, {"for": "de", "rel": "new", "text": "German plurals are irregular (Buch → Bücher, Tisch → Tische) and learned per noun; Chinese has nothing to learn here."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "new", "text": "Hindi marks the plural on nouns and verbs (लड़का → लड़के); Chinese marks it nowhere, except 们 for groups of people."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "similar", "text": "Persian -hā is left out after numbers (se ketāb ‘three books’); Chinese leaves the plural out always, and 们 is only for people."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "new", "text": "Marathi plurals change the noun according to its gender class (मुलगा → मुलगे, पुस्तक → पुस्तके); Chinese nouns never change."}, {"for": "ar", "rel": "new", "text": "Arabic has sound and broken plurals (kitāb → kutub) and a dual; Chinese has neither: 书, 两本书, 三本书."}, {"for": "he", "rel": "new", "text": "Hebrew plurals in -im / -ot (sefer → sfarim) agree with adjectives and verbs; Chinese marks no plural at all."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Turkish -lar / -ler is dropped after numbers (iki kitap ‘two book’); Chinese treats every noun like that, all the time."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "same", "text": "Japanese also leaves nouns unmarked (本 is ‘book’ or ‘books’) and has -たち for groups of people (子供たち ~ 孩子们); the two systems are very close."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "similar", "text": "Korean -들 is optional and mostly used for people (학생들 ~ 学生们); Chinese 们 is narrower still: never on things, never after a number."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "new", "text": "Finnish marks the plural in every case (kirja → kirjat, kirjoissa); Chinese never changes the noun."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hungarian keeps the noun singular after numbers (három könyv ‘three book’), but has a plural -k elsewhere (könyvek); Chinese keeps the noun bare everywhere."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "new", "text": "Swahili marks the plural with the class prefix (kitabu → vitabu, mtu → watu); Chinese has no prefixes and no plural."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "new", "text": "Luganda marks the plural by changing the class prefix (omuntu → abantu, ekitabo → ebitabo); Chinese 人 is one person or many."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "same", "text": "Vietnamese nouns do not change either; những / các before a noun mark plurality when needed, where Chinese uses 这些, 一些 or 很多."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "new", "text": "Inuktitut distinguishes singular, dual and plural on the noun (inuk, inuuk, inuit); Chinese marks none of them."}]}, "de": {"fn.definite": [{"for": "el", "rel": "similar", "text": "ο / η / το and ένας / μία / ένα change for gender and case as der / ein do. Greek also puts the article before names and with possessives (ο Γιάννης, το σπίτι μου); standard German does not (Anna, mein Haus)."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "new", "text": "Russian has no articles; German makes you choose der or ein for every singular noun – known or specific (der), new or any (ein)."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "similar", "text": "el / la and un / una are familiar, but German articles change for case too, and there is no plural of ein (Spanish unos): Das sind Stühle."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "the / a work the same way, but the German article also shows gender, number and case: der, die, das, den, dem, des."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "similar", "text": "le / la / les, un / une are familiar, but German has no plural indefinite (French des) and no partitive: Ich esse Brot (je mange du pain), Das sind Stühle (ce sont des chaises)."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "new", "text": "Hindi has no articles (ek is sometimes used for “a”); German needs der or ein before almost every singular noun."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "different", "text": "Persian marks “a” with -i or yek and a definite object with rā; German uses a separate article before the noun: ein Buch, das Buch."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "new", "text": "Marathi has no articles; German has der / die / das and ein / eine, which also show the gender and case."}, {"for": "ar", "rel": "different", "text": "The Arabic article al- is a prefix that never changes, and an indefinite noun takes -un (kitābun); German uses separate words that change with gender and case: das Buch, ein Buch, den Tisch."}, {"for": "he", "rel": "different", "text": "Hebrew ha- is a prefix for every gender and number, and “a” has no word (sefer = a book); German needs der / die / das and ein / eine."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "different", "text": "Turkish has bir for “a” but no definite article; definiteness of an object shows in the accusative (elmayı – the apple, elma – an apple). German uses der / ein in every case."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "new", "text": "Japanese has no articles; German needs der or ein, and the choice tells the listener whether the thing is already known."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "new", "text": "Korean has no articles; German needs der or ein before a singular noun."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "new", "text": "Finnish has no articles; German needs der or ein before nearly every singular noun."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "similar", "text": "a / az and egy are articles like der and ein, but they never change; German articles change for gender, number and case."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "new", "text": "Swahili has no articles; German makes you choose der or ein, which also carries gender and case."}, {"for": "zh", "rel": "new", "text": "Chinese has no articles (这 / 那 or 一个 when needed); German needs der or ein before nearly every singular noun."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "new", "text": "Vietnamese has no articles; German needs der / ein before nearly every singular noun."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "new", "text": "Inuktitut has no articles; German needs der or ein, chosen by whether the thing is known."}], "fn.overview": [{"for": "el", "rel": "similar", "text": "Three genders and cases will feel familiar, but the genders often differ (ο τοίχος – die Wand, το πιάτο – der Teller, η καρέκλα – der Stuhl), and German has a dative where Greek uses σε + accusative or the genitive. German never drops the subject pronoun (Ich bin Lehrerin, not just *Bin Lehrerin), and its word order is strict: the verb is second. Many learned words go back to Greek: Telefon, Schule (σχολή), Lampe (λαμπάς)."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "English is German’s close cousin: Haus – house, Buch – book, Mann – man, Glas – glass, Tür – door. But English lost genders, cases and most endings and keeps subject–verb–object order; German puts the verb second or last and has no helper “do” (Bist du Lehrer? – Are you a teacher?). Beware false friends: Handy (mobile phone), Gift (poison), bekommen (to get, not “become”)."}, {"for": "de", "rel": "same", "text": "If German is your own language, this course shows it from the outside – as a fusional language with gender, cases and verb-second order – and checks the details even fluent speakers slip on: the dative plural -n (den Kindern), weak nouns (den Jungen, dem Studenten), measure nouns such as zwei Glas Bier, and register (Sie or du, Guten Tag or Hallo)."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "similar", "text": "Russian is fusional too: three genders, cases and tables are familiar ideas. But German has four cases (Russian six), and it shows the case mostly on the article, which Russian does not have – der / ein will be the real novelty. Russian leaves out “to be” in the present (я студент); German cannot (ich bin Student). ты / вы match du / Sie, but вы is also the plural of ты, where German has a separate ihr."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "different", "text": "Turkish has no gender and no definite article (only bir), so der / die / das and agreement are new. Cases are a familiar idea (-i, -e, -de, -den), but in German they sit mostly on the article, not on the noun. There is no vowel harmony: a German ending has one shape (Lehrerin, Köchin; Zeitung, Wohnung), unlike -ler / -lar. Turkish helps elsewhere: ö and ü sound much as in German, compounds stack words as Turkish stacks suffixes, the verb at the end of German subordinate clauses feels Turkish, and sen / siz match du / Sie."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "similar", "text": "Articles and gender are familiar, but German has three genders, and its articles also change for case (der, den, dem, des). The subject pronoun is never left out (Ich komme, not *Komme), and one verb, sein, covers both ser and estar. tú / usted correspond to du / Sie, vosotros to ihr."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Articles, gender and obligatory subject pronouns are familiar; new are the third gender (das), the four cases on the article and the verb-second order. ü and ö are the vowels of French tu and peu; German has no nasal vowels – the n of Bahnhof or Zentrum is always said. tu / vous correspond to du / Sie, but German has a separate plural ihr."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "different", "text": "Hindi has gender (two), a verb that agrees with the subject, and verbs at the end – German has three genders, puts the verb second in a main clause and last only in subordinate clauses, and uses prepositions before the noun where Hindi has postpositions after it (ghar mẽ – im Haus). German has articles (der, ein), Hindi none. The subject of a German verb is always in the nominative, also in the past – there is no ergative ne. German p, t, k are breathy, but that breath never separates two words as kh and k do in Hindi."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "different", "text": "Persian has no grammatical gender – even u means he and she – so der / die / das, er / sie / es and agreement are new. German has articles where Persian has the suffix -i (ketābi, a book) and rā for a definite object. Possession is not linked with an ezafe (ketāb-e man) but with a word before the noun: mein Buch. tu / šomā match du / Sie."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Marathi also has three genders, a neuter included, so der / die / das is a familiar idea – but the gender of a given noun has to be learned anew. German puts the verb second in a main clause, not at the end, and uses prepositions before the noun instead of endings and postpositions after it. German wir is one word for “we”: it does not separate “we including you” (āpaṇ) from “we without you” (āmhī)."}, {"for": "ar", "rel": "different", "text": "German builds words from stems and endings, not from roots and patterns: Buch – Bücher – Bücherei share a stem, and the vowel change Buch → Bücher (umlaut) is the nearest thing to a pattern. German has three genders, no dual, and an article that is a separate word changing with gender and case (der, den, dem). sein is never left out. German h is a light breath, and there are no pharyngeal or emphatic consonants: t, s, d have no “heavy” partners. Printed German letters stand apart and keep one shape wherever they are in the word."}, {"for": "he", "rel": "different", "text": "Words are built from stems and endings, not roots and patterns. German has three genders and an article that changes with gender, number and case (der, die, das, den, dem …) instead of the one prefix ha-; it also has an indefinite article (ein). sein is always there in the present (Das Haus ist groß), and the verb ending shows the person, not the gender. German letters have no final forms: n, m, k look the same at the end of a word."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "new", "text": "Almost everything in the noun phrase is new: articles, three genders, number and four cases shown on the article. Instead of particles after the noun (ga, o, ni) German changes the article; the verb comes second, not last, and it changes for the person (ich gehe, du gehst). Politeness lives in the choice du / Sie, not in verb forms such as -masu. Pitch never changes a German word – the stress does."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "new", "text": "Articles, gender, obligatory plural and the case endings of the article are new, and the verb stands second in a main clause, not at the end. There are no speech levels in the verb: politeness is the pronoun Sie and the matching verb form (Kommen Sie?)."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "different", "text": "Cases are familiar, but German has only four and shows them mostly on the article; German has gender (Finnish hän is he and she), articles (Finnish none) and many prepositions where Finnish uses case endings. No vowel harmony (an ending has one shape) and no consonant gradation: a German stem keeps its consonants (Buch, Buches), only the vowel may change (Bücher)."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "different", "text": "Articles are familiar (a / az – der, egy – ein), but German articles change for gender and case. German has three genders (Hungarian none), four cases instead of a long row of suffixes, no vowel harmony, and stress that is not always on the first syllable (Kaˈffee, Toˈmate). The verb does not change for a definite object: ich sehe das Haus / ein Haus."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "new", "text": "German nouns have three genders, not a long row of noun classes, and the gender is shown on the article and the adjective, rarely on the noun itself (der Tisch, die Tür, das Buch). The verb takes person endings at its end (ich gehe, du gehst) instead of prefixes, objects are separate words (ich sehe ihn), and German has nothing like the applicative: “cook for someone” is kochen für + noun."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "new", "text": "German nouns have three genders, marked on the article (der, die, das) and not by a class prefix on the noun; the plural is an ending or a vowel change (Buch – Bücher), not a new prefix. The verb takes person endings at its end, and pitch is intonation only: it never changes a German word."}, {"for": "zh", "rel": "new", "text": "German is the opposite pole: words change their form all the time (ich gehe, du gehst; der, den, dem), there are no tones – pitch only shapes the sentence – and no measure words (zwei Bücher). Gender, number and case must be chosen for every noun. Doubling a word changes nothing in German (看看, 慢慢 have no German pattern): ein bisschen, langsam."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "new", "text": "Words change their form (gehe, gehst, geht; Buch, Bücher), pitch never changes a word, and there are no classifiers. German syllables can begin and end with clusters of consonants (Strumpf, Herbst), which need practice."}, {"for": "cy", "rel": "different", "text": "German never changes the first sound of a word: Katze stays Katze in meine Katze, seine Katze, ihre Katze – where Welsh says fy nghath, ei gath, ei chath. Only the endings and the vowel inside the word change."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "different", "text": "German words are long only as compounds of nouns (Haustür, Kleiderschrank); a sentence is still several words, and the verb does not take in its object – German has no incorporation. Every subject is in the nominative, the subject of a transitive verb included (no ergative), and there is no antipassive: the object is simply left out (Ich esse)."}], "fn.plural.noun": [{"for": "el", "rel": "similar", "text": "Greek also has several plural endings by gender and declension (-οι, -ες, -α), but never changes the vowel inside the word; German often does: Buch → Bücher, Stuhl → Stühle."}, {"for": "ru", "rel": "similar", "text": "Several endings (-ы, -и, -а) are familiar, but German adds umlaut (Buch → Bücher) and has no special forms after numbers: zwei Bücher, fünf Bücher."}, {"for": "es", "rel": "different", "text": "Spanish adds -s / -es; German has five patterns, often with umlaut, so the plural is learned with each noun: der Stuhl, die Stühle."}, {"for": "en", "rel": "similar", "text": "English -s is one German pattern (Handys, Babys), and man – men, foot – feet show the same old vowel change as Mann – Männer; in German that change is everywhere: Buch – Bücher, Haus – Häuser."}, {"for": "fr", "rel": "different", "text": "In French the plural is mostly heard on the article (le / les); German shows it on the article (die) and usually on the noun too: der Tisch – die Tische."}, {"for": "hi", "rel": "similar", "text": "Hindi plurals depend on gender and ending (laṛkā → laṛke, kitāb → kitābẽ), as German ones depend on gender and ending; German adds umlaut and five patterns."}, {"for": "fa", "rel": "different", "text": "Persian has one main plural, -hā (ketābhā), and keeps the noun singular after numbers; German has five patterns and always uses the plural after zwei."}, {"for": "mr", "rel": "similar", "text": "Marathi plurals also depend on the noun’s gender and ending; German adds the vowel change (umlaut): Buch – Bücher."}, {"for": "ar", "rel": "different", "text": "Arabic broken plurals change the vowels inside the word (kitāb → kutub); German umlaut does something similar on a small scale (Buch → Bücher). German has no dual (kitābāni = zwei Bücher) and no singulative: “a tree” is ein Baum, not a form derived from the collective."}, {"for": "he", "rel": "different", "text": "Hebrew plural -im / -ot is regular, and pairs and some time words take a dual (yomayim); German has five patterns and no dual: zwei Tage."}, {"for": "tr", "rel": "different", "text": "One plural suffix in Turkish (-ler / -lar); German has five patterns – learn the plural with each noun – and needs the plural after numbers too (zwei Bücher, iki kitap)."}, {"for": "ja", "rel": "new", "text": "Japanese usually leaves number unmarked (hon = book or books); in German a plural noun must change: Buch – Bücher, Tisch – Tische."}, {"for": "ko", "rel": "new", "text": "Korean -deul is optional; the German plural is obligatory and has five patterns."}, {"for": "fi", "rel": "different", "text": "Finnish has one regular plural (-t, -i-: talot, taloissa); German has five patterns and the umlaut, learned with each noun."}, {"for": "hu", "rel": "different", "text": "Hungarian has one plural, -k with a linking vowel (házak), and uses the singular after numbers; German has five patterns and uses the plural after zwei."}, {"for": "sw", "rel": "different", "text": "Swahili makes the plural by changing the class prefix (kitabu → vitabu, mtu → watu); German changes the end of the word, often the vowel too (Buch → Bücher), and the article becomes die."}, {"for": "lg", "rel": "different", "text": "The plural is a different class prefix (omuntu → abantu, ekitabo → ebitabo); German changes the ending and often the vowel (Buch → Bücher) and uses die for every plural."}, {"for": "zh", "rel": "new", "text": "Chinese nouns do not change for number (们 only for people); every German plural noun changes, and must be learned: Tisch – Tische, Buch – Bücher."}, {"for": "vi", "rel": "new", "text": "Vietnamese marks plural with những / các before the noun if at all; German changes the noun itself (Buch – Bücher) and the article (die)."}, {"for": "iu", "rel": "different", "text": "Inuktitut has singular, dual and plural endings (inuk, inuuk, inuit); German has no dual – two people are zwei Menschen, with the ordinary plural."}]}}""")
